<?xml version="1.0" standalone="yes"?>

<!--
secretsman — central secret storage for Unraid Docker templates.
https://github.com/kmbrimble/unraid-secretsman

Not distributed via Community Applications. Install by URL from
Plugins -> Install Plugin, using this file's raw.githubusercontent.com URL.
See README.md and CLAUDE.md "Shipping model" for the release process, and
RECOVERY.md before you install this on a system you can't afford to reboot
into a broken state without a plan.
-->

<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "unraid-secretsman">
<!ENTITY author    "kmbrimble">
<!ENTITY version   "2026.08.25">
<!ENTITY launch    "Settings/SecretsMan">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kmbrimble/unraid-secretsman/main/unraid-secretsman.plg">
<!ENTITY gitURL    "https://github.com/kmbrimble/unraid-secretsman/releases/download/v&version;">
<!ENTITY plgPATH   "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY emhttp    "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY md5       "87c964fc93952e85b9ac09a730c2bddb">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;" support="https://github.com/kmbrimble/unraid-secretsman" min="7.0" icon="lock">

<CHANGES>
###2026.08.25
- Fixed: restore gave no feedback at all — confirmed live the backend was already returning a
  correct result (added/collisions), so the gap was client-side: restore is the only action
  using fetch() directly (needed because FormData bodies can't safely go through Unraid's
  global CSRF $.ajaxPrefilter), and its X-CSRF-Token header path had never been exercised
  end to end. Now also appends csrf_token as a form field, the same primary mechanism every
  other action already proves works, plus explicit HTTP-status error surfacing instead of
  relying solely on JSON-parse failure. Restore now reports what happened in the banner:
  merge names how many keys were added (and which) versus already present (and which,
  values never shown); replace now also reports which keys were removed (added
  removed-key tracking to secretsman_backup_restore(), which previously only tracked
  additions). A restore also now refreshes the main secrets table, not just the backup
  section. Same audit turned up that Save backup settings and Back up now gave no
  confirmation either — both now show a banner (Back up now names the archive path and
  size). md5 bumped to match.
- Fixed from the first real browser round trip: the destination directory picker never
  opened, because SecretsMan.page linked jquery.filetree.css but never loaded
  jquery.filetree.js itself - $.fn.fileTree was simply undefined. Added the missing
  &lt;script&gt; tag, the same way appdata.backup's own settings.php does it. Also
  constrained the archive-password field (was full page width, pushing its "show"
  toggle off-screen, same class of layout bug as the earlier add-secret Value field) and
  added a macOS-specific line to README-RESTORE.txt: the built-in Archive Utility refuses
  a 7z/AES-256 archive and reports it as damaged, which reads exactly like a wrong
  password at the worst possible moment - The Unarchiver or Keka work instead. md5 bumped
  to match.
- Fixed backup_cron_main() (backup_cron.php) pre-emptively before it broke the same way:
  it had the identical STDOUT/STDERR-under-php-fpm shape as the bug just fixed below, and
  was safe only because backup_now (backup_api.php) duplicated its logic instead of calling
  it — resolving that duplication the natural way would have reintroduced the exact bug.
  Converted to the same return-a-result shape and had backup_now call it directly, so the
  duplication is gone without the trap. apply_patch_main()/uninstall_main() have the same
  shape and are deliberately left as-is (no web-context caller exists or is planned) — see
  CLAUDE.md rule 8. md5 bumped to match.
- Fixed a live 500 on saving backup settings: backup_cron_register.php's fwrite(STDOUT/STDERR)
  calls fatal ("Undefined constant STDOUT") when run in-process from the web request that
  saves the schedule, since those constants are CLI-SAPI-only and undefined under php-fpm —
  the function returns a result now instead of printing. Also broadened both backup_api.php's
  and store_api.php's dispatch to catch Throwable, not just SecretsmanError, so an unhandled
  fatal surfaces a readable message in the page banner instead of a bare 500 the page could
  only describe as "check the browser console". Fixed secretsman_notify()'s own STDERR
  fallback (common.php) to use error_log() pre-emptively, since the same bug class is now
  reachable through it too. Also: plugin description and Support Thread link now show on the
  Installed Plugins page (README.md was never packaged; added a support= attribute), the
  backup schedule row no longer overflows the page width (the mode/weekday selects had no
  width class against Unraid's default select{width:100%}), the destination field now has a
  real directory picker (jquery.filetree.js + webGui/include/FileTree.php are genuinely
  shared/stock, not appdata.backup-private — only its convenience wrapper was), and the
  retention default is 3, not 30 (an emergency-restore mechanism, not version history; 3 not
  2 so one bad backup doesn't evict the last known-good archive). md5 bumped to match.
- Backup &amp; restore, before 1.0.0: encrypted archives of the secret store (7z/AES-256 when
  p7zip is available — not stock Unraid — falling back to tar + openssl AES-256-CBC/PBKDF2
  otherwise, with an HMAC-SHA256 sidecar closing the tamper-detection gap plain CBC leaves
  since this host's openssl refuses AEAD ciphers), scheduled via a proper flash .cron file +
  update_cron (regenerated every boot from backup-config.json, not trusted-as-is — the exact
  fix appdata.backup's own changelog documents needing after losing schedules to a reboot),
  manual "Back up now", download-most-recent, and a two-mode restore (replace, or merge
  add-only with collisions named and never silently resolved). The archive password lives
  beside store.json (outside flash, root:root 0600) rather than in it (circular) or on flash
  (defeats the whole plugin's premise) — documented plainly that root on this host can already
  read store.json directly regardless, so the password's real job is protecting the archive
  once it leaves this host. Every archive is decrypt-and-validate verified immediately after
  creation, before being considered successful. New: src/backup.php, scripts/backup_api.php,
  scripts/backup_download.php, scripts/backup_cron.php, scripts/backup_cron_register.php.
  md5 bumped to match.
- Fixed (step 7, pre-1.0.0 verification): the Method="remove" block gated the Helpers.php
  revert on `[[ -x scripts/uninstall.php ]]`, but that script (like every plugin script) has
  no shebang and is invoked as `php &lt;path&gt;` — it's packaged 0644 and never chmod +x, so the
  check was always false and the revert silently never ran. Invisible by accident: a reboot
  restores stock Helpers.php anyway, so "removal worked" looked true either way. Changed to
  `-f`. Regression-tested in tests/run.php. md5 bumped to match.
- Added visible page-face instructions (what/why, token format, Copy Token workflow) above
  the add form, with finer detail (grammar, what's not protected, store location) kept behind
  the (?) expander — one line behind an expander wasn't enough for a first-time user.
- Polish pass on SecretsMan.page (presentation only): action buttons right-aligned, a
  revealed value wraps within its cell instead of pushing into the buttons, an in-use
  indicator (tick/cross) column next to "Used by", left-aligned column headers, an
  expandable help block using the stock inline_help convention, generic add-form
  placeholders, namespace group headings with real visual weight, and a clearer empty
  state. md5 bumped to match.
- Fixed: store_api.php's own explicit CSRF check could never succeed — Unraid's
  auto_prepend_file (webGui/include/local_prepend.php) already enforces CSRF on every plugin
  POST and consumes ($_POST['csrf_token'] is unset) the token once it validates, so a second
  check downstream always saw an empty field and rejected every legitimate request with a
  403. Removed the redundant check entirely; relies solely on Unraid's own enforcement now,
  matching the ipmi plugin's stock precedent. Also fixed the overlapping "show" checkbox
  label in the add-secret Value field. md5 bumped to match.
- Phase 3: a Settings page (SecretsMan.page, under Settings -> User Utilities) for managing
  the store without hand-editing store.json over SSH — add/edit/delete secrets, a per-row
  Reveal (never prefilled, never rendered into the page source unrevealed), a Copy-token
  button that puts the exact "!secret ns/key" on the clipboard, and usage awareness that
  scans saved templates for tokens with no matching store entry or that don't parse at all.
  All validation and file handling reuses src/secretsman.php's existing functions (the
  token grammar, secretsman_load_store()'s shape/newline checks) as the single source of
  truth — nothing is re-implemented. &launch; updated to point at the new page. See
  CLAUDE.md's Phase 3 entry for the full design and why a corrupt store is diagnosed, never
  auto-repaired, by this page. NOT PUBLICLY RELEASED; md5 bumped to match.
- Removed !secretfile entirely. Its !secret-only benefit (hiding a value from
  docker inspect / /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/environ) required root or docker-socket access
  the store is equally readable with, and keeping it required a boot-ordering
  guarantee that a live test disproved (see CLAUDE.md's dated correction). A
  !secretfile token now aborts container creation, naming !secret instead. The
  event/ directory, scripts/repopulate.php, and scripts/force_start.php are
  gone with it — nothing in this .plg references them anymore. md5 bumped to
  match the smaller package; NOT PUBLICLY RELEASED, staged for the same Gate-2
  verification as everything else in this file.
- Fixed: the packaged .txz's internal paths were relative to the package name
  (unraid-secretsman/...) instead of the real absolute destination
  (usr/local/emhttp/plugins/unraid-secretsman/...). Slackware's upgradepkg extracts
  relative to /, not relative to any "plugins" convention, so this landed everything
  at /unraid-secretsman instead — confirmed as the exact cause of a live Gate-2 boot
  failure (apply_patch.php "Could not open input file", plugin install exit 1). See
  scripts/build-plugin.sh and docs/phase2-resume.md for the full diagnosis. md5 bumped
  to match the corrected package; regression-tested in tests/run.php.
- Phase 2: patch layer, boot-time re-patch, !secretfile repopulation at array start.
  NOT PUBLICLY RELEASED — no GitHub Release has been published. The &name;-&version;.txz
  and &name;.md5 this .plg expects were staged directly on flash at &plgPATH; for Gate-2
  live/reboot verification only (see docs/phase2-resume.md). The installer (see
  dynamix.plugin.manager/scripts/plugin, "skipping: $name already exists") never
  contacts &gitURL; while those local files are present and their hash matches — so
  this .plg has NOT been tested against the actual download path. Do not assume the
  URL fetch works until a real release is cut and tried.
</CHANGES>

<FILE Name="&plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz">
<URL>&gitURL;/&name;-&version;.txz</URL>
<MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="&plgPATH;/&name;.md5">
<INLINE>&md5;</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
set -e

# md5-verify before touching anything, same pattern as every other .plg
# on this system (see reference in CLAUDE.md "Boot-time patch pattern").
sum1=$(/usr/bin/md5sum &plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz | awk '{print $1}')
sum2=$(/usr/bin/cat &plgPATH;/&name;.md5)
if [[ "$sum1" != "$sum2" ]]; then
  echo "secretsman: package md5 mismatch, refusing to install"
  rm -f &plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz
  exit 1
fi

upgradepkg --install-new &plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz

# Unpacks to &emhttp; (src/, scripts/, reference/, SecretsMan.page), matching
# the repo's plugin/ tree exactly (src/ and reference/ are symlinked into
# plugin/ in the repo for local testing; the packaged txz contains real
# files, not symlinks — see scripts/build-plugin.sh). SecretsMan.page needs
# no chmod or separate registration — Unraid's page loader picks up any
# *.page file sitting in the plugin's installed root automatically.

# Re-applies (or verifies, or refuses+notifies) the Helpers.php patch.
# Runs on every install AND every boot (rc.local re-installs every .plg
# in /boot/config/plugins on every boot — see CLAUDE.md). Idempotent:
# see src/patch.php secretsman_patch_is_applied().
php &emhttp;/scripts/apply_patch.php

# Regenerates the backup schedule's flash .cron file from backup-config.json
# and re-registers it with crond, every boot — /etc/cron.d is Unraid's RAM
# rootfs (wiped every boot; confirmed live), so only the flash .cron file
# persists, and appdata.backup's own changelog documents losing exactly this
# ("Scheduling got lost after reboot") from trusting a stale copy instead of
# regenerating. update_cron only touches /boot/config and /etc/cron.d — no
# array dependency — so this needs no separate event/disks_mounted hook.
php &emhttp;/scripts/backup_cron_register.php

echo "secretsman: install complete"
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
# Cleanly reverts Helpers.php to stock immediately, rather than making
# the user reboot to get it back (a reboot alone would also fix it —
# /usr/local/emhttp is never persisted — see RECOVERY.md).
if [[ -f &emhttp;/scripts/uninstall.php ]]; then
  php &emhttp;/scripts/uninstall.php || true
fi

# Deregister the backup schedule BEFORE rm -rf &plgPATH; below deletes the
# flash .cron file this depends on — done here, not left to update_cron
# alone, since nothing else would ever tell crond the schedule is gone.
rm -f &plgPATH;/&name;.cron
update_cron || true

removepkg &name;-&version;
rm -rf &emhttp;
rm -rf &plgPATH;

echo "secretsman: removed, Helpers.php reverted to stock"
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
